<header><p class="st-kicker">kingdom · Maritime Southeast Asia</p><h1 class="st-title">Luwu Kingdom</h1><p class="st-meta">1300 CE – 1905 CE (approx.) · capital Palopo</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The oldest Bugis kingdom in tradition, setting of the I La Galigo epic and source of the iron used for South Sulawesi's weapons.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Luwu, at the head of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gulf-of-bone">Gulf of Bone</a>, was regarded by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bugis">Bugis</a> as their senior kingdom, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bantustan">homeland</a> of the heroes of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/la-galigo">I La Galigo</a> cycle, one of the longest epics in the world. It probably rose in the 14th century, controlling iron ore from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lake-matano">Lake Matano</a> area and trade from the interior highlands.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Luwu was ruled by a datu, chosen from a sacred lineage. Its nickel-rich iron was prized for weapons and sold widely in the archipelago. In 1605 its ruler <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/la-patiware-daeng-parabung">La Patiware Daeng Parabung</a> became one of the first in South <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sulawesi">Sulawesi</a> to accept <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a>, taught by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/minangkabau-people">Minangkabau</a> preachers.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Luwu lost ground to the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/bone-sultanate">Bone Sultanate</a> and the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/gowa-sultanate">Gowa Sultanate</a> from the 16th century and came under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> control after their conquest of South <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sulawesi">Sulawesi</a> in 1905. Its datu <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/andi-djemma">Andi Djemma</a> backed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indonesia">Indonesian Republic</a> in 1945 to 1946 and is now a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/national-hero-of-indonesia">National Hero of Indonesia</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><div class="st-emblem" role="img" aria-label="kingdom, 1300 CE – 1905 CE (approx.)"><svg viewBox="0 0 100 100" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M22 72 V40 L36 54 L50 28 L64 54 L78 40 V72 Z"/><path d="M22 80 H78"/><circle cx="50" cy="24" r="3"/><circle cx="22" cy="36" r="2.5"/><circle cx="78" cy="36" r="2.5"/></svg><p class="st-emblem-type">kingdom</p><p class="st-emblem-region">Maritime Southeast Asia</p><div class="st-span" aria-hidden="true"><span class="st-span-bar" style="left:85.57%;width:12.04%"></span></div><div class="st-span-axis" aria-hidden="true"><span>3000 BCE</span><span>1 CE</span><span>2000</span></div></div><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1300 CE – 1905 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/14th-century">14th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>kingdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Palopo</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>La Patiware Daeng Parabung, Andi Djemma</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Sunni Islam</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Bugis</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/indonesia">Indonesia</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Luwu, Kedatuan Luwu</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1602&amp;focus=luwu-kingdom" aria-label="Luwu Kingdom on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="8017 2290 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Luwu Kingdom lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M8337 2546L7959 2663"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M7959 2663h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M8337 2546h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M8337 2546h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Palopo. <a href="/atlas?year=1602&amp;focus=luwu-kingdom">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

